For Homework 1 and 2, I used Agile TDD, where I would write the test cases 
first, then implement code and test it, upload to Github, then continue. 
For this assignment, I used something much more in line with waterfall. 
I wrote most of the code, then tested, then implemented the last few things.

This method of development had its pros and cons. I am much more familiar
with Waterfall as that was my main method of coding for my entire academic
career. However, for long code that is several hundred lines, TDD is better.

This program came out to around 180 lines.

I started by implementing the switch cases for the -e and -n flags, then I 
added all of the easy outputs that did not require extra code like 
class, uid, username, etc.

I then wrote the logic for everything else, checking how many groups a given
user was a part of, checking when a user would expire, etc.

One specific issue I encountered was that I was unable to compile on my local
system, so I had to do all of my initial testing on the ssh server. 

Some parts of my code caused compilation issues locally, like the
system specific user and group information. While the program compiled on
the NetBSD server, differences between macOS and NetBSD meant that some
parts would have needed to be changed for my local system.

Towards the end of the assignment, while doing all my checking, I was
reviewing my code and changed the hardcoded limit of 128 groups to be
dynamically allocated by malloc, that way it 1) avoids magic numbers
and 2) in the event of a user belonging to more than 128 groups, my
code would be able to handle that.

Overall, this assignment was not too challenging and was a good practice to 
develop actual usable software.